e is seen as more  likely  but they could be wrong and they know is  there a chance we might never find out  the true origin of covet 19  i think that it's easier to destroy  evidence in a laboratory and in  databases than it is in the wild  so if it turned out that there really  wasn't evidence  for a zoonotic spillover but there also  wasn't evidence for lab leak i suspect  that would  edge me towards believe towards thinking  things when we're all nefarious  but you know absence of evidence isn't  evidence of absence you know if you  can't find it in  the world you may just not be looking in  the right places and  looking hard enough and evolution will  come and get you  the question of the origin of  covid19 you know a pandemic that has  killed potentially um upwards of about  10 million people  is it's a really compelling question and  one that i've  kind of felt slightly obsessed with uh  you know  since the start of last year and  you know it's hard to know whether we're  going to get to a resolution  um but nonetheless you know it is a  question that will  keep coming up until we do get good  answers  and it's it's sort of taking on uh  you know a bit of the aura isn't it of  things like you know who shot kennedy  and  um you know how marilyn monroe died it's  become a very much a sort of iconic  question  certainly of our time um and i think you  know as the  months and years go on if it if it  hasn't  been sufficiently well answered we'll  continue to sort of reverberate  um you know over the decades until um  you know the next one comes along  i'm natasha loda the health policy  editor of the economist  you can read our coverage of covid19 by  clicking on the link  thanks for watching and don't forget to  subscribe

